window.PIGEON_INDEX=[{"u":"index.html","h":"Overview","p":"Overview","t":" Pigeon connects the Codex sessions already running on your Mac to a private Slack conversation. Ask what the agent is doing, steer it, approve a command, or answer its question without going back to your desk. Preview, macOS only. Tested against Codex CLI 0.169.0 and its shared daemon 0.169.1. The package is not yet published to npm and the repository is being prepared for publication, so installation is from a source checkout. See what is verified, experimental, and unsupported . "},{"u":"index.html#start-here","h":"Start here","p":"Overview","t":" Start here # Getting started # Requirements, install, first message. About ten minutes. Slack setup # Create the Slack app from a manifest, with an annotated diagram for every click. Using Pigeon # What to type, and what Pigeon does with it. Troubleshooting # Symptom, cause, command to run. "},{"u":"index.html#how-the-pieces-fit","h":"How the pieces fit","p":"Overview","t":" How the pieces fit # Pigeon is a small local service. It speaks Slack over an outbound Socket Mode connection and talks to Codex through the shared local daemon that Codex itself already runs. There is no cloud component and no second model. You → Slack DM → Pigeon (on your Mac) → existing Codex daemon → your original session ← observed activity, requests, results ← Two things are worth knowing up front, because the rest of the docs build on them: Messages are routed by simple, deterministic rules. A fixed set of phrases is answered locally from observed state. Everything else goes to Codex as an instruction. Read the exact rules. Pigeon talks to live sessions only. A saved conversation is not a live connection. Sessions and recovery explains what that means. "},{"u":"index.html#about-the-screenshots","h":"About the screenshots","p":"Overview","t":" About the screenshots # Slack's developer pages need a signed-in browser. These docs label every visual honestly: Real rendering means it was rendered from Pigeon's actual markup or output, and Illustrative diagram means it is a drawing of a Slack page with numbered callouts, not a captured screenshot. Slack's labels change over time; if a button is worded differently, follow the highlighted position and the text in the step. "},{"u":"getting-started.html#what-you-need","h":"What you need","p":"Getting started","t":" What you need # Requirement Why macOS Credentials go in the login Keychain and Pigeon runs as a LaunchAgent. Windows and Linux are not supported. Node.js 24 or newer, and npm package.json sets engines.node =24 . Codex, signed in, with a reachable shared daemon Pigeon joins the daemon. codex app-server daemon version should report it. Permission to install a custom Slack app Needed once, in the workspace you want to message from. "},{"u":"getting-started.html#install-from-source","h":"Install from source","p":"Getting started","t":" Install from source # The npm name pigeon belongs to a different project. Do not run npx pigeon . The planned scoped name @pigeon-dev/cli is unpublished. terminal $ git clone https://github.com/Murat-Barkin-Kinay/pigeon.git # once the repository is public $ cd pigeon $ npm ci $ npm run build $ node dist/cli.js setup If you already have a checkout, skip the clone. To get a pigeon command on your PATH instead of node dist/cli.js , run npm pack and then npm install -g ./pigeon-dev-cli-0.1.0.tgz . "},{"u":"getting-started.html#what-setup-does","h":"What setup does","p":"Getting started","t":" What setup does # Starts a local page on 127.0.0.1 (random port and path, expires after 20 minutes) and opens it. It shows the Slack app manifest and two token fields. You create the Slack app, install it, and paste two tokens. Slack setup shows each click. Pigeon validates both tokens with Slack, stores them in Keychain, and prints a one-time line: In a direct message to Pigeon, send: pair code . You send that line to Pigeon in Slack. Pairing locks Pigeon to your user and workspace. Pigeon installs a LaunchAgent ( dev.pigeon.companion ), registers its MCP server with Codex and installs its skill. You can close the terminal. Real rendering · The page pigeon setup opens, rendered from the shipped markup and manifest. The fields are empty. "},{"u":"getting-started.html#your-first-conversation","h":"Your first conversation","p":"Getting started","t":" Your first conversation # Open Codex normally in a project and give it a task. Keep it running. In Slack, open your DM with Pigeon and send sessions . Pigeon lists live sessions with a Connect button each. Press the one you want. Reply in that thread: What are you doing? The message that triggered the selector is not sent after you choose. Send your message again in the thread. Separate threads keep separate projects. "},{"u":"getting-started.html#check-the-install","h":"Check the install","p":"Getting started","t":" Check the install # terminal $ node dist/cli.js doctor doctor reports Slack pairing, Keychain items, the background service, the Codex runtime and live session count, skill discovery, MCP tool verification, and whether the Slack Socket Mode credential is still valid. If something is wrong, see Troubleshooting . "},{"u":"getting-started.html#uninstall","h":"Uninstall","p":"Getting started","t":" Uninstall # terminal $ node dist/cli.js uninstall Removes the LaunchAgent, local runtime and state, the two Keychain items, and the skill, MCP registration and instruction paragraph Pigeon added (files you modified are preserved and reported). It does not delete the Slack app (remove that in Slack) or touch other Codex settings or history. "},{"u":"slack-setup.html","h":"Slack setup","p":"Slack setup","t":" Slack makes you do this once. The manifest sets every scope, event, and Socket Mode switch for you, so the manual part is: create, install, copy two tokens. Why not one click? Slack requires the app to be installed and a Socket Mode token to be generated by a person in the browser. Pigeon does not fake an OAuth flow, and it does not ask for your Slack password or an app-configuration token. "},{"u":"slack-setup.html#before-you-begin","h":"Before you begin","p":"Slack setup","t":" Before you begin # You ran node dist/cli.js setup , so the local page is open. Keep it open; you will paste into it at the end. You are signed in to Slack in your browser, in the workspace where you want to chat with Pigeon. Slack Free allows ten installed apps; Pigeon uses one. Open the Slack developer apps page # Go to api.slack.com/apps and choose Create New App . Illustrative diagram · Step 1: the Create New App button. Choose the manifest option # Slack words this as From a manifest ; Pigeon's setup page calls it “Paste a manifest”. Either way, it is what removes most of the manual configuration. Illustrative diagram · Step 2: pick the manifest route instead of building from scratch. Pick the workspace and paste the manifest # Select your workspace, then paste the JSON from the local setup page (or from slack-manifest.json in the repository). Choose JSON if Slack offers a format switch. Review the summary and create the app. slack-manifest.json { \"display_information\": { \"name\": \"Pigeon\", \"description\": \"Your coding agent is one message away.\", \"background_color\": \"#26443b\" }, \"features\": { \"bot_user\": { \"display_name\": \"Pigeon\", \"always_online\": false }, \"app_home\": { \"home_tab_enabled\": false, \"messages_tab_enabled\": true, \"messages_tab_read_only_enabled\": false } }, \"oauth_config\": { \"scopes\": { \"bot\": [\"chat:write\", \"im:history\"] } }, \"settings\": { \"socket_mode_enable"},{"u":"slack-setup.html#what-each-permission-is-for","h":"What each permission is for","p":"Slack setup","t":" What each permission is for # Permission Used for chat:write (bot) Posting the agent's messages and the approval buttons. im:history (bot) Receiving message.im events from your DM with the bot. connections:write (app-level) Opening the Socket Mode WebSocket. Not requested: channel history, search, user directory, file upload, admin scopes, a signing secret, a public webhook, or a user OAuth token. The bridge also drops any event that is not a direct message from your paired user and workspace. "},{"u":"slack-setup.html#refreshing-credentials","h":"Refreshing credentials","p":"Slack setup","t":" Refreshing credentials # Rerun setup . It stops the service during the flow and keeps your pairing if the workspace is unchanged. Leave the app-token field blank to keep the saved one. A different workspace needs pairing again. Slack interface reference: the diagrams above were drawn from Slack's documented setup flow for apps created from a manifest. They have not been captured from a live signed-in session, so wording may differ slightly. "},{"u":"codex-integration.html#how-pigeon-finds-codex","h":"How Pigeon finds Codex","p":"Codex integration","t":" How Pigeon finds Codex # Pigeon does not launch Codex and does not scrape a terminal. It connects to the shared Codex app-server daemon over the local control socket under CODEX_HOME (default ~/.codex ), using a WebSocket over a Unix socket. Nothing listens on a TCP port. It lists threads that are loaded in that daemon ( thread/loaded/list ). Internal subagent threads and threads that are no longer loaded are left out. When you press Connect , it joins that thread with thread/resume on the same server, without overriding model, directory, or permissions. If the control socket is unavailable it may run Codex's own idempotent app-server daemon start , at most once per 30 seconds. It never upgrades, restarts, stops, or enables remote control on Codex. terminal $ codex app-server daemon version # should report a reachable daemon $ node dist/cli.js sessions # JSON list of live, controllable sessions "},{"u":"codex-integration.html#supported-surfaces","h":"Supported surfaces","p":"Codex integration","t":" Supported surfaces # Surface Status Codex CLI opened normally, using the shared daemon Verified Tested on 0.169.0 / daemon 0.169.1 IDE sessions that share the daemon Experimental A VS Code-sourced session was visible. No claim for every extension version. Dedicated Codex desktop app Experimental Not tested CLI started with --no-daemon , or any private/embedded server Not built Not reachable: no shared control endpoint An older Codex client can fall back to a private embedded runtime even while a daemon exists. doctor tells you whether Pigeon can see a shared runtime, not whether a given window is attached to it. If a window is missing from sessions , that is the first thing to suspect. "},{"u":"codex-integration.html#mcp-tools-and-the-skill","h":"MCP tools and the skill","p":"Codex integration","t":" MCP tools and the skill # setup (and install-agent ) gives the agent a way to reach you on its own initiative. All of it is reversible and tracked by a receipt in Pigeon's home directory. What Where MCP server pigeon Registered with codex mcp add . A locally owned launcher runs pigeon mcp from Pigeon's immutable installed runtime. Skill ~/.agents/skills/pigeon/SKILL.md Awareness block A !-- pigeon:begin -- … !-- pigeon:end -- section in AGENTS.md (or AGENTS.override.md if that exists) under CODEX_HOME . If a pigeon skill or MCP entry already exists that Pigeon did not create, or you edited the block, setup refuses and preserves it rather than overwriting. New Codex sessions load the awareness; existing ones may need a restart to see the skill. The four tools # Tool Use pigeon_get_status Read-only: is Slack connected and paired, and what voice preferences apply. pigeon_notify_human Queue a short update without blocking. “Queued” is not “delivered”. pigeon_ask_human Ask a question and wait for the correlated reply. Pending requests survive; reuse request_id . pigeon_request_human_action Ask for an outside action, such as approving a Microsoft Authenticator number. The reply is a human report, not proof; the agent must verify. Codex's own tool consent still applies. The first use of a sending tool may need local approval, and a non-interactive session with approval policy never c"},{"u":"codex-integration.html#limits-to-know","h":"Limits to know","p":"Codex integration","t":" Limits to know # Pigeon lists live sessions only. A conversation saved on disk is not a live connection. See Sessions and recovery . There is no atomic “join only if already loaded” call in the public protocol. In a narrow race a thread can unload between the check and the join; Codex may then cold-resume it. A successful join of a continuously loaded thread was demonstrated; a universal guarantee is not claimed. After a Codex daemon restart, Pigeon reconnects only to loaded identities and invalidates old approval controls. This is experimental. "},{"u":"using-pigeon.html","h":"Using Pigeon","p":"Using Pigeon","t":" Talk to it like a colleague. A small set of phrases is answered from what Codex has already done. Everything else is passed to your original session. "},{"u":"using-pigeon.html#ask-what-it-is-doing","h":"Ask what it is doing","p":"Using Pigeon","t":" Ask what it is doing # Recognised phrases include what are you doing , how's it going , status , are you stuck , how are the tests going , and what's failing (also in Spanish and Turkish). Pigeon reads the latest turn and replies. No model turn is created and nothing is interrupted. Counts and failing test names appear only when they exist in observed test output. Otherwise Pigeon passes your question to Codex rather than invent an answer. "},{"u":"using-pigeon.html#give-an-instruction","h":"Give an instruction","p":"Using Pigeon","t":" Give an instruction # Anything not recognised as a special phrase is an instruction, for example Focus on latency first. If a turn is active, Pigeon steers it with turn/steer , guarded by that turn's ID. If the session is idle, it starts the next turn in the same session. Pigeon confirms (“Passed that to Codex on …”) only after Codex accepts the input. This includes free-form questions such as “why did you pick that library?”. They are delivered to the working session as input; there is no separate side conversation. See routing . "},{"u":"using-pigeon.html#pause","h":"Pause","p":"Using Pigeon","t":" Pause # Pause , stop or interrupt asks Codex to interrupt the exact active turn. It is not a checkpoint: a terminated command is not restored. If Codex is disconnected, the stop is rejected, never queued for later. "},{"u":"using-pigeon.html#continue","h":"Continue","p":"Using Pigeon","t":" Continue # Continue is simply a new instruction in the same session. "},{"u":"using-pigeon.html#ask-to-be-told-when-it-finishes","h":"Ask to be told when it finishes","p":"Using Pigeon","t":" Ask to be told when it finishes # Let me know when it's finished watches the exact current turn. If Codex is already idle, Pigeon says so and suggests asking for the latest result. "},{"u":"using-pigeon.html#approve-a-request","h":"Approve a request","p":"Using Pigeon","t":" Approve a request # When Codex asks to run a command, Pigeon posts the project directory, the complete command and the reason, with Approve once and Deny buttons. Typing “yes” does nothing: only the button counts. Controls expire after 10 minutes, and are invalidated by reconnects or by a local decision. Codex still applies its own policy. File-change approvals, broad permission bundles, truncated commands, and unknown request types stay in Codex, and Pigeon tells you to look there. "},{"u":"using-pigeon.html#answer-a-question-from-the-agent","h":"Answer a question from the agent","p":"Using Pigeon","t":" Answer a question from the agent # When Codex asks one non-secret question (Plan mode), reply in the thread to resolve that exact request. Secret fields and multi-question forms stay local. When the agent calls pigeon_ask_human or pigeon_request_human_action , your reply goes back to the waiting call. Example: an Authenticator prompt Pigeon Quick one — Microsoft is asking for number 88. Can you approve that? You Done. Pigeon Passed that back to Codex on agentlane. Approve in Microsoft's own app. Pigeon never touches Authenticator, and the agent is told to verify that sign-in really succeeded. "},{"u":"using-pigeon.html#what-gets-a-notification","h":"What gets a notification","p":"Using Pigeon","t":" What gets a notification # proactiveness You get quiet Human requests, failures and interruptions, and anything you explicitly asked for. important (default) The above plus completed turns. updates Also progress, at most once a minute per session. "},{"u":"using-pigeon.html#change-the-voice","h":"Change the voice","p":"Using Pigeon","t":" Change the voice # terminal $ node dist/cli.js voice $ node dist/cli.js voice set humour off $ node dist/cli.js voice set proactiveness quiet $ node dist/cli.js voice set language tr # en, es or tr $ node dist/cli.js voice reset Options: formality (casual, neutral, formal), humour (dry, off), verbosity (brief, normal, detailed), proactiveness , language , address . The running service picks changes up within five seconds. "},{"u":"using-pigeon.html#several-projects","h":"Several projects","p":"Using Pigeon","t":" Several projects # Each Slack thread is bound to one exact Codex session. Send sessions , choose, and keep talking in that thread. A stale binding fails closed: Pigeon tells you the session went offline and sends nothing. It never falls through to another project. "},{"u":"routing.html","h":"How messages are routed","p":"How messages are routed","t":" Pigeon's rule is that a casual question should not disturb working code, and an approval should never be a guess. Here is how that is actually implemented today. No conversational fork yet. An earlier design idea was to answer questions in a forked copy of the session. That is not in this codebase. The sections below describe what exists. Not built "},{"u":"routing.html#the-routing-table","h":"The routing table","p":"How messages are routed","t":" The routing table # You send Intent What happens Interrupts work? “What are you doing?”, “status” status Reads the session's latest turn and replies from observed state. No new turn. No sessions , help sessions Posts a list of live sessions with Connect buttons. No “pause”, “stop” interrupt turn/interrupt on the exact active turn. Yes, by request “let me know when it’s finished” watch Stores a watch on the current turn ID. No “done”, “yes”, “ok” ambiguous Used only if it answers a pending question; otherwise Pigeon asks what to pass along. No Anything else instruction turn/steer if a turn is active; turn/start if idle. Adds input to the active turn "},{"u":"routing.html#interactive-view","h":"Interactive view","p":"How messages are routed","t":" Interactive view # status phrase fixed list → Observed-state reply Local read. Discussion without execution. anything else instruction → Original Codex session Steer the active turn or start the next one. Codex native request from Codex, not you → Approvals One-use button, bound to request, session, thread and expiry. question in a forked session → Not built Not built No fork code exists. Free-form questions currently go to the original session. resume a stopped session → Not built Not built No recovery manager. Pigeon only sees live sessions. "},{"u":"routing.html#why-status-is-answered-locally","h":"Why status is answered locally","p":"How messages are routed","t":" Why status is answered locally # Asking “what are you doing?” while Codex is mid-task is the most common message. Reading state creates no turn and no user item, which was verified with a real Slack message (the in-progress turn ID was unchanged). The phrase list is deliberately small and deterministic, so Pigeon never claims to understand more than it does. "},{"u":"routing.html#what-this-means-for-free-form-questions","h":"What this means for free-form questions","p":"How messages are routed","t":" What this means for free-form questions # “What's the bottleneck?” is not a recognised phrase, so it is an instruction and reaches the working session. If Codex is mid-turn it enters as steering input. It is better to treat Pigeon as a way to talk to the one agent, not as a separate assistant that is aware of the work. "},{"u":"routing.html#fail-closed-routing","h":"Fail-closed routing","p":"How messages are routed","t":" Fail-closed routing # A thread with an existing binding uses that session or fails. A stale binding never falls through. With no binding, exactly one live session is selected automatically. Several produce a selector. Pigeon does not guess. Selecting a session does not send the message that prompted the selector. If delivery cannot be confirmed (for example Pigeon crashed mid-send), the instruction is marked uncertain and is never replayed . Pigeon tells you to check Codex first. Queued instructions expire after five minutes. "},{"u":"sessions.html","h":"Sessions and recovery","p":"Sessions and recovery","t":" No Session Recovery Manager. Pigeon cannot resume a stopped conversation for you. It can only reconnect to sessions that are still loaded in the running Codex daemon. Not built "},{"u":"sessions.html#four-states-one-rule","h":"Four states, one rule","p":"Sessions and recovery","t":" Four states, one rule # State Can Pigeon talk to it? Active : loaded in the daemon, a turn running Yes. Status, steering, interruption, approvals. Idle : loaded in the daemon, waiting Yes. Instructions start the next turn in the same session. Saved : history on disk, not loaded No. It is not listed and cannot be selected. Interrupted : a turn you paused The session is still live and idle. The turn is not restored. The rule: a saved conversation is not a live connection. Resuming history may restore the conversation but not a terminated process at the exact instruction where it stopped. "},{"u":"sessions.html#what-pigeon-does-restore","h":"What Pigeon does restore","p":"Sessions and recovery","t":" What Pigeon does restore # Pigeon restarts ( Verified ): thread-to-session bindings return and Pigeon rejoins the same live session, with no second worker. Slack reconnects: Socket Mode reconnects automatically; Pigeon catches up the latest agent message of the latest turn. Unsent notifications wait in a durable outbox. Crash during delivery: marked uncertain, warned in the thread, never replayed. Codex daemon restarts ( Experimental ): Pigeon retries discovery and reconnects only to loaded identities; old approval buttons are invalidated. "},{"u":"sessions.html#if-your-session-has-stopped","h":"If your session has stopped","p":"Sessions and recovery","t":" If your session has stopped # Reopen it in Codex (for example resume the conversation from the Codex CLI or app) so it is loaded again in the shared daemon. DM sessions to Pigeon and press Connect on it. Send your instruction in the thread. What you will see when the old session is gone You Continue AgentLane. Pigeon That session has gone offline. I haven’t sent your message. Send “sessions” to reconnect explicitly. "},{"u":"sessions.html#sleep-and-network-loss","h":"Sleep and network loss","p":"Sessions and recovery","t":" Sleep and network loss # SDK reconnection and the durable outbox are implemented, but physical sleep and network-drop tests have not been run. Slack is not a guaranteed offline inbox. Experimental "},{"u":"troubleshooting.html","h":"Troubleshooting","p":"Troubleshooting","t":" Start with node dist/cli.js doctor (or pigeon doctor ). It checks pairing, Keychain, the service, the Codex runtime, the skill, MCP tools, and the Slack credential. "},{"u":"troubleshooting.html#setup-and-slack","h":"Setup and Slack","p":"Troubleshooting","t":" Setup and Slack # Pigeon does not respond in Slack Run doctor . Is the background service running and the Slack credential valid? Did you pair? status says “not paired” until you DM the pair … line from your account. Are you in the Messages tab of the Pigeon app? Pigeon ignores channels and edited messages. Are you messaging from the same workspace and Slack user you paired? terminal $ node dist/cli.js status $ node dist/cli.js logs App token problems (“could not validate or securely store these tokens”) The app token must start with xapp- and carry only connections:write . The bot token must start with xoxb- and come from an installed app. Setup validates the bot token with auth.test and the app token with apps.connections.open ; the terminal prints which stage failed ( bot-validation , app-validation , keychain-bot , keychain-app ). bot-validation : reinstall the app and copy the token again. app-validation : regenerate the app-level token with connections:write ; check Socket Mode is enabled. keychain-* : see the Keychain item below. Missing OAuth permissions Under OAuth Permissions → Bot Token Scopes you need chat:write and im:history . Under Event Subscriptions the bot event message.im . If you edited the app, the simplest repair is to compare against slack-manifest.json (App Manifest page), then reinstall the app and run setup again. Socket Mode disconnects, or “Slack di"},{"u":"troubleshooting.html#codex-and-sessions","h":"Codex and sessions","p":"Troubleshooting","t":" Codex and sessions # Codex session not found / “I can’t see a live Codex session” Open Codex normally and start a task. Check codex app-server daemon version . The window may use a private or embedded server, another CODEX_HOME , a custom executor, or --no-daemon . History alone does not make a session controllable. “Daemon package missing”: install a supported official Codex distribution. Pigeon does not repair managed packages. terminal $ node dist/cli.js sessions “That session has gone offline” The bound session is no longer loaded. Pigeon sent nothing. Reopen it in Codex, then DM sessions and Connect again. See Sessions and recovery . Main session cannot be controlled Controls apply only to a thread loaded in the shared daemon. A second app-server cannot take over a thread: Codex rejects it with an active-writer error, and Pigeon never bypasses that lock. Use the session from the client that owns it, or reopen it through the shared daemon. Ownership conflict or “Pigeon is already running” An atomic lock prevents two Pigeon consumers. Stop the service before pairing manually, and do not run daemon while the LaunchAgent is active. “Conversational fork unavailable” There is no fork feature in this release, so there is nothing to enable. Free-form questions go to the original session. See routing . Skill not discovered, or MCP registration failed doctor prints skillDiscovered "},{"u":"troubleshooting.html#approvals-and-delivery","h":"Approvals and delivery","p":"Troubleshooting","t":" Approvals and delivery # Pending approval expired or unavailable Controls last 10 minutes and are invalidated by a Pigeon reconnect, a local approval, or a Codex restart. Unsupported requests (file changes, extra permissions, truncated commands) are never offered. Review it in Codex. “I couldn’t confirm delivery” Pigeon will not replay a possibly-delivered instruction. Look at the session in Codex, then resend if needed. Queued messages expire after five minutes; offline stop requests are never queued. Duplicate or old replies after reconnect Notifications are at-least-once. A crash after Slack accepted a post can duplicate it. It cannot cause duplicate Codex execution. "},{"u":"status.html","h":"Supported, experimental, unsupported","p":"Supported, experimental, unsupported","t":" Evidence collected on 2026-11-18: macOS 15.5 arm64, Node 24.18.0, Codex CLI 0.169.0, shared daemon 0.169.1. This page follows docs/COMPATIBILITY.md and docs/TESTING.md in the repository. "},{"u":"status.html#verified","h":"Verified","p":"Supported, experimental, unsupported","t":" Verified # Discover a normally opened CLI session, then attach to the same live worker (same thread and turn IDs). Slack → original session → Slack, with a real human Slack message. Steer an active turn; interrupt the exact active turn. Status replies that add no turn and no user item. Human command approval (one-time) and a single-question structured input. Proactive agent messages after you select a session. Pigeon restart rejoining the same live session; LaunchAgent bootstrap and restart. Clean package install into an empty prefix. "},{"u":"status.html#implemented-with-restrictions","h":"Implemented with restrictions","p":"Supported, experimental, unsupported","t":" Implemented with restrictions # Status recognition is a fixed phrase list, not natural-language understanding. Completion means the end of a Codex turn, not an arbitrary milestone. Approvals: a single displayable command with no additional permission bundle. Reconnect catch-up covers the latest turn only. Multiple sessions work with separate threads. No stress-scale result. The agent-side MCP tools and skill: a fresh Codex session independently read the skill and sent a real Slack message ( docs/awareness-notify-proof.json ). They also work from private runtimes such as codex exec ; steering does not. "},{"u":"status.html#experimental","h":"Experimental","p":"Supported, experimental, unsupported","t":" Experimental # The Codex daemon protocol itself and cross-version behaviour. Recovery after a Codex daemon restart. Mac sleep and network loss. The dedicated Codex desktop app and IDE extensions beyond the observed case. "},{"u":"status.html#not-built","h":"Not built","p":"Supported, experimental, unsupported","t":" Not built # A conversational fork for answering questions beside the main session. A session recovery manager that resumes saved conversations. Remote attachment to private stdio or embedded runtimes; terminal injection. File-change and broad permission approvals from Slack; multi-question forms. Slack file uploads and screenshots. Windows and Linux installation. npm publication ( @pigeon-dev/cli is a proposed, unpublished name). "},{"u":"status.html#cost","h":"Cost","p":"Supported, experimental, unsupported","t":" Cost # Pigeon runs on your Mac with SQLite and Slack Socket Mode. There is no cloud worker, tunnel, hosted database, or separate model API. Codex's own usage remains yours. Slack Free allows ten apps and shows the latest 90 days of history. "},{"u":"status.html#measured-on-one-development-machine","h":"Measured on one development machine","p":"Supported, experimental, unsupported","t":" Measured on one development machine # Metric Observation Live session discovery 3.2–6.5 ms (10 samples) Slack instruction → delivery acknowledgment about 1.39 s (one conversation) Idle service about 121 MiB RSS, 0.3% CPU (one sample) These are single observations, not performance guarantees. Raw data is in docs/*-proof.json . "},{"u":"architecture.html","h":"Architecture","p":"Architecture","t":" Pigeon is a companion to a runtime that already owns the agent. Slack input crosses a transport boundary into the same Codex thread the normal UI uses. "},{"u":"architecture.html#data-flow","h":"Data flow","p":"Architecture","t":" Data flow # Inbound on the top row; replies, approvals and notifications return along the bottom row through the durable outbox. "},{"u":"architecture.html#modules","h":"Modules","p":"Architecture","t":" Modules # Path Responsibility src/transports/slack/ Bolt Socket Mode bridge and authenticated ingress (pairing, DM-only, workspace/user check). src/router.ts classify() into status, sessions, interrupt, watch, ambiguous or instruction; selectSession() without guessing. src/runtimes/codex/ CodexRpc (WebSocket JSON-RPC) and CodexAdapter (sessions, snapshot, attach, deliver, interrupt). src/approvals.ts Native command approvals and single-question input as Slack controls. src/store.ts SQLite: bindings, inbox/outbox, approvals, watches, bounded conversation context. src/conversation/ Voice and personality, status replies, notification policy. src/agent/ MCP server, tool contracts, local IPC, the human-communication coordinator, skill and installer, verifier. src/daemon/ Runtime staging under ~/.pigeon/runtimes/ and the LaunchAgent. src/security/keychain.ts macOS Keychain access. "},{"u":"architecture.html#agent-initiated-path","h":"Agent-initiated path","p":"Architecture","t":" Agent-initiated path # The MCP process never holds Slack credentials or Codex control. Codex supplies the thread ID with each call. "},{"u":"architecture.html#runtime-contract","h":"Runtime contract","p":"Architecture","t":" Runtime contract # Joining uses thread/resume on the same server after a loaded check. The protocol has no atomic “subscribe only if loaded”. docs/UPSTREAM.md proposes one. Status reads metadata and the latest turn only. Steering requires expectedTurnId ; a turn change makes it fail and it is never retried as turn/start . Inbound messages are stored before Slack's envelope is acknowledged; events are deduplicated by ID. Instructions move queued → sending → done ; a crash in sending becomes uncertain . Outbound posts are chunked, paced at one per second, retried with backoff. Delivery is at-least-once. "},{"u":"architecture.html#not-in-the-code","h":"Not in the code","p":"Architecture","t":" Not in the code # There is no conversation fork, no main-agent controller distinct from the adapter, and no session recovery manager. The longer repo doc is docs/ARCHITECTURE.md . "},{"u":"security.html","h":"Security","p":"Security","t":" A paired Slack identity can instruct Codex with the permissions Codex already has. Treat that Slack account, its logged-in devices, the bot token, and your macOS account as trusted control surfaces. This preview has not had an independent security audit. "},{"u":"security.html#what-pigeon-accesses","h":"What Pigeon accesses","p":"Security","t":" What Pigeon accesses # Codex's local control socket, to list threads, read recent turns, steer, interrupt, and answer native requests. Your direct messages with the Pigeon bot only. Its own SQLite database and logs in ~/.pigeon . "},{"u":"security.html#what-is-sent-to-slack","h":"What is sent to Slack","p":"Security","t":" What is sent to Slack # Raw agent messages, command text for approvals, status excerpts, and questions. These can contain source code or other private content. Only bind projects whose output you are willing to send to Slack. Pigeon does not detect or redact secrets in model output. Slack keeps its own copies. "},{"u":"security.html#how-tokens-are-stored","h":"How tokens are stored","p":"Security","t":" How tokens are stored # As macOS Keychain generic-password items dev.pigeon.slack.bot and dev.pigeon.slack.app . Values are passed to Keychain on stdin, not in process arguments. There is no plaintext fallback. The setup page binds to 127.0.0.1 on a random port and path, checks Host and Origin, requires a CSRF value, forbids framing, and expires after 20 minutes. "},{"u":"security.html#how-authorization-works","h":"How authorization works","p":"Security","t":" How authorization works # Pairing needs a random phrase shown only in your terminal. After that, only direct messages from that user in that workspace are accepted. Wrong users, other channels, edits and bot events are rejected before anything is stored. "},{"u":"security.html#how-approvals-are-handled","h":"How approvals are handled","p":"Security","t":" How approvals are handled # Each control is one-use and bound to the connection generation, the exact native request ID, the thread, the Slack conversation and a 10-minute expiry. Typing “yes” is never an approval. A local decision, reconnect or expiry invalidates the control. Pigeon never approves automatically. File changes, permission bundles, truncated commands and unknown requests cannot be approved remotely. "},{"u":"security.html#local-retention","h":"Local retention","p":"Security","t":" Local retention # The database is mode 0600 inside a 0700 directory. Completed message bodies are cleared. One bounded last question and reply per Slack thread is kept for follow-ups until the binding changes or you uninstall. This is bounded retention, not forensic erasure. "},{"u":"security.html#what-pigeon-does-not-do","h":"What Pigeon does not do","p":"Security","t":" What Pigeon does not do # No TCP control port, tunnel, or hosted component. No scraping of memory or terminals, key injection, rollout edits, or lock bypass. No access to Microsoft Authenticator or any identity provider. The agent must verify real sign-in state. No collection of your Slack password, a user OAuth token, signing secret, or app-configuration token. No collection of passwords, OTPs, recovery codes or tokens through the MCP tools. "},{"u":"security.html#revoking-access","h":"Revoking access","p":"Security","t":" Revoking access # pigeon uninstall removes the service, local state and Keychain items. Remove the Slack app in Slack to revoke the tokens at the source. Report vulnerabilities privately; no reporting endpoint is configured until the repository is published, so do not post secrets or exploit details publicly. "},{"u":"contributing.html#develop","h":"Develop","p":"Contributing","t":" Develop # terminal $ npm ci $ npm run check # tsc --noEmit $ npm test # tsx --test tests/*.test.ts $ npm run format:check $ npm run build Tests never need Slack tokens or a Codex login. They include a real Unix WebSocket fixture, out-of-order RPC correlation, crash recovery without replay, expired queues, ingress authorization and one-use approval controls. Scripts in scripts/ are opt-in experiments against a real Codex and consume normal usage; read them first. "},{"u":"contributing.html#rules-that-keep-pigeon-trustworthy","h":"Rules that keep Pigeon trustworthy","p":"Contributing","t":" Rules that keep Pigeon trustworthy # A runtime adapter exposes only what it can observe and control. Never substitute a resumed transcript for a running worker, retry an uncertain instruction, broaden a security policy, or choose an ambiguous session. Add a regression test for any routing, authorization, persistence or protocol fix. Never put private session logs, local config, tokens or unredacted screenshots in issues, fixtures or commits. "},{"u":"contributing.html#work-on-this-website","h":"Work on this website","p":"Contributing","t":" Work on this website # terminal $ node website/build.mjs # regenerates website/docs/*.html and the search index $ python3 -m http.server -d website 8000 # preview at http://localhost:8000 Docs content lives in website/src/docs/*.html , and the Slack diagrams in website/src/shots.mjs . The site uses only relative links, so it works from a GitHub Pages project subpath. Replacing a diagram with a real screenshot: save it under website/assets/screenshots/ , redact tokens, workspace names and personal data first, and reference it with img:file.png|alt|caption . "}];window.PIGEON_BASE="";
